polaris — POLARIS board CLI (run from anywhere inside the repo)
  init-board                     create board dirs, gitignore .polaris/ + the board set, lock dir
                                 (board state commits to refs/heads/polaris/board — base stays clean)
  pack [ID]                      YOUR WHOLE CONTEXT FOR ONE TASK, IN ONE CALL — run this FIRST,
                                 before reading anything: the task's Why + acceptance, its contract
                                 verbatim, the repo's DETECTED house style, files you own vs may
                                 only read, the code-map for each owned directory, the public API
                                 surface you must not break, the known traps recorded against those
                                 exact paths, and the verify: commands that will be run. Read-only.
                                 No ID inside a feat/<ID> worktree = that task. Replaces the 6-15
                                 exploratory round trips a cold session otherwise spends
  claim [ID]                     lock + ready→active + worktree  (no ID = top wsjf; skips locked)
  resume [ID]                    re-enter an already-claimed active task (crash/kickback/new session)
  verify [ID]                    diff ⊆ files_owned + run verify: commands (in worktree)
  handoff [ID]                   verify + push + active→review   (lock stays)
  release <ID> [--to ready|blocked] [-m note]   abort: give the task back cleanly
  grant <ID> <path> -m "why"     append one path to a CLAIMED task's files_owned (sanctioned
                                 amendment; REFUSES any overlap with another ready/active task's
                                 ownership — refusal mutates nothing; RULES.tsv still binds)
  kickback <ID> [-m note]        Integrator: red merge → review→active
  audit <ID>                     Integrator: ownership check of feat/<ID>
  run-verify <ID>                Integrator: re-run task verify: commands in CWD
  done <ID>                      Integrator: review→done + map_delta as docs(map): on base + cleanup
  task-commit-msg <task-file>    print the task's landed-commit message (pure; `land` consumes it)
  land <ID>                      Integrator, on integrate/<date>: audit + squash feat/<ID> into
                                 ONE rich commit ([<ID>] subject + Landed-from trailer; no board write)
  land --express <ID>            ONE pass for a single small task, from <base>: integrate branch +
                                 audit/land + ONE full suite (red → unwind + kickback) + seal +
                                 run-verify + done + branch cleanup. Refuses: another task in
                                 review/ · risk: high · express: off · publish: pr. Every gate of
                                 the long path still runs; `finish` stays the finish line.
  seal [<date>]                  Integrator: seal the wave — merge integrate/<date> into base
                                 (--no-ff, per-task bullets) + lightweight tag sprint/<n>; a later
                                 wave's seal MOVES the tag (sprint's latest sealed checkpoint).
                                 publish: pr (CONVENTIONS key, default direct): NO local merge —
                                 pushes ONLY integrate/<date> + prints the PR-create URL
  seal --sync [<date>]           publish: pr only — finish the wave after the human merges the PR
                                 (MERGE COMMIT strategy, never squash): verify every [<ID>] subject
                                 reached base, tag sprint/<n>, delete integrate/<date> local+remote
  report [--sprint <n> | --all]  render the management-readable per-sprint record (docs/sprints/
                                 by default) from the board + history; writes the file(s), prints
                                 the path(s), commits nothing (seal commits it on the wave instead)
  history [--tasks <n>]          changelog view of base (board noise hidden); --tasks <n> drills
                                 into one sealed sprint's task commits, across all its waves
  rollback <ID|sprint/<n>>       revert one landed task (or the sprint's latest sealed wave, -m 1)
                                 on base — forward-only: never resets, never force-pushes
  status [--brief] | sweep [--fix] | doctor [--selftest [--only <patterns>] [--parallel <N>]]
                                 status --brief = ONE plain-English paragraph, no table (plain status
                                 unchanged): Last landed: / Next up: markers + a sprint-column clause
                                 doctor --selftest --only <patterns> = the always-on spine + only the
                                 labeled drills matching any comma-separated glob (fmlist tcm report
                                 metrics brain rules drift hardening qa finish remote syncrace notify
                                 grant upgrade pr-publish express hint brief); a nonsense element dies
                                 before the spine (unknown drill label); --parallel <N> = run the
                                 selected drills as N parallel child shards (serial stays the default)
  check [--only <glob>] [--update]
                                 golden-output acceptance tests, ZERO LLM: ops/tests/<name>.cmd is
                                 run and its stdout diffed against <name>.expected (optional
                                 <name>.rc = expected exit code). --update rewrites goldens — a
                                 human/Builder decision, never automatic. Wire it into qa by setting
                                 `uat: bash ops/polaris check` in ops/CONVENTIONS.md
  check --scaffold [--app] [--cmd "<shell>"]
                                 GENERATE the pairs from observed behavior — public API surface per
                                 source dir (from the index) + this CLI's parsed output shapes. Runs
                                 each candidate TWICE and discards any that flaps, is empty, or whose
                                 command is missing. Never overwrites an existing golden. --cmd adds
                                 ONE behavioural golden for your own command (auto-running an app's
                                 executables would start servers, so it is opt-in). --app locks the
                                 HOST app's shape instead: declared dependencies (Invariant 8 as a
                                 diff), npm script names, HTTP routes, migration set, and referenced
                                 env var NAMES — all by reading tracked files, never by running the
                                 app. REVIEW the output: a golden records what the code DOES, not
                                 what it SHOULD do.
  harness [--refresh]            GENERATE ONE runnable test suite for the HOST APP — the behaviour
                                 tier above `check --scaffold --app` (which only locks shape by
                                 reading files, and so cannot tell you the app still BOOTS).
                                 Detects the stack, writes <tests>/test_polaris_harness.py or
                                 polaris-harness.test.js, and captures a baseline inventory.
                                 Three sweeps: every module IMPORTS · every GET route answers
                                 non-5xx · every declared entry point runs --help without crashing;
                                 plus "nothing disappeared" against the baseline. A sweep that
                                 cannot find what it needs SKIPS and says why — it never passes by
                                 asserting nothing, and it never invents an app factory's args.
                                 Written once by an agent, then costs a subprocess and ZERO tokens
                                 forever. Wire into `uat:` so qa picks it up. --refresh regenerates
                                 the file and re-captures the baseline (how you accept a real change)
  find <symbol> | -f <glob> | -t <text> | --importers <path> | --imports <path> | --api <glob> [-n N]
                                 the 1-hop "where is X": ONE line per hit, path:line first, ranked
                                 (exact > prefix > substring, boosted by churn + fan-in, tests and
                                 vendor down-weighted). Backed by a generated .polaris/index.db;
                                 self-heals on query. No python3 → says so and you fall back to Grep
  show <path>#<symbol> | <path>:<line>
                                 print JUST that symbol's body — not the whole file
  board-fm [<col>…]              one TAB line per task (col id pts wsjf risk deps owns contract
                                 title) — the Planner's board read without the prose bodies.
                                 Default columns: ready active backlog blocked (done/ is history,
                                 pass it explicitly). Non-task files (IDEAS.md) are skipped
  brain [--refresh]              generate .polaris/brain/ — gitignored knowledge base a cold agent
                                 reads FIRST (INDEX + code-map/board/contracts/commands/gotchas);
                                 --refresh skips code-map when code is unchanged. seal auto-refreshes
                                 an existing brain; doctor warns when it is stale
  metrics                        opens with a plain-English summary line, then cycle · throughput ·
                                 kickbacks · build-vs-integrate split (EVENTS.ndjson)
  why <ID>                       why a task bounced/blocked: kickback + release/blocked notes from telemetry + body
  notify-gate <kind> [ID]        fire the notify: hook at a human gate, and nothing else — kinds:
                                 plan · risk <ID> · question <ID> · done [ID]  (observe-only:
                                 never writes the board, EVENTS.ndjson, or any lock)
  triage                         which LANE this board's work belongs in — line 1 is one word:
                                 solo (one context, zero subagents; 1 task ≤3pts, risk normal,
                                 express on, publish direct) · express (one task, one builder,
                                 one integrator) · full (everything else). Reasons follow on
                                 stderr-style note lines. Decide with this, never by re-reading
                                 the board and weighing the six conditions by hand.
  drift [--strict]               mechanical board-hygiene audit (overlap · ready gate · cruft · stale refs)
  rules                          list + health-check ops/RULES.tsv (repo policy as data)
  qa [--force]                   is everything okay, in ONE shot: test/lint/typecheck/build/uat
                                 from CONVENTIONS + drift --strict + doctor env check. Runs every
                                 check even after a red; rc 1 if anything was red.
                                 SKIPS the suite when HEAD is unchanged since the last green run
                                 and the tree is clean (.polaris/suite-stamp) — drift and doctor
                                 still run every time. --force always re-runs the suite.
                                 `finish` runs this for you at the close.
  finish [--force]               is the RUN over? The LAST command of every lane. Nothing building,
                                 nothing waiting to land, ready/ drained per drain:, no unmerged
                                 integrate/<date>, no orphan lock, clean tree on base, qa green.
                                 rc 0 = complete (the notify: done hook fires, exactly once per
                                 finished state) and the agent may open its reply with the
                                 `# 🎉 Complete!` H1; rc 1 names every pending thing, and there is
                                 no H1. `caveat:` lines are not gates — the close must mention them.
  dash [--port N]                live read-only board at http://127.0.0.1:7373 (needs python3)
  fleet <N> [--loop] [--launch] [--dry-run]
                                 print N Builder kickoffs; --launch opens a session per task in
                                 tmux windows or Windows Terminal panes (--dry-run previews the command)
  upgrade                        migrate an OLD BOARD (v3/v4→v5). Idempotent. Downloads NOTHING.
                                   want a newer KIT? that is `update` — one letter, unrelated job.
  slim [--apply | --restore]     the per-context TOKEN TAX, measured: every skill/agent/command
                                 definition under ~/.claude injects its name+description into the
                                 system prompt of every session AND every subagent it spawns,
                                 invoked or not. Reports bytes per family, marks the ones no
                                 POLARIS role/CLAUDE.md/PROTOCOL.md ever names, and totals what a
                                 6-8 context run pays for them. --apply MOVES those into
                                 ~/.claude/.polaris-archived/ (never deletes); --restore puts them
                                 all back. Report-only by default; the installer never applies it
  version                        installed version · commit · latest on the channel
  update [--repo-only]           fetch the latest KIT from the channel (manual; never automatic).
                                   also re-caches it into ~/.claude so the NEXT repo you install
                                   into gets the new kit too. --repo-only skips that.
  uninstall --yes                remove POLARIS from this repo (keeps your CLAUDE.md + hooks)
